ITEMS=[]
def Q(stem,key,d1,d2,d3,why):ITEMS.append((stem,[key,d1,d2,d3],why))
Q("An owner discovers that a firearm has been stolen, but the licence card is still safely at home. Which reporting duty applies?",
 "Report the theft to police within 24 hours","Report the lost card to the Registrar","Notify the Registrar within 30 days","Report it at the next licence renewal",
 "Section 120(11) requires reporting the firearm's theft within 24 hours after becoming aware of it, to the police station nearest to where it occurred.")
Q("A wallet holding the licence card is stolen, but the licensed firearm remains secure. Which report is required even though no firearm is missing?",
 "Tell the Registrar within 24 hours","Report a firearm theft to the police","Nothing, since the firearm is safe","Apply to renew the licence early",
 "Section 29 requires the holder to notify the Registrar of the loss or theft of the licence document within 24 hours of discovery.")
Q("A person finds cartridges hidden in a park that clearly belong to nobody lawfully, and knows exactly where they are. What does section 120(2) require?",
 "Tell police where they are at once","Take them home and hand them in later","Report them to police within 30 days","Report only firearms, not ammunition",
 "Section 120(2) requires reporting the location to a police official without delay. It does not authorise taking possession.")
Q("Information supplied in an approved application changes, with no loss or theft involved. What must the holder do?",
 "Notify the Registrar within 30 days","Report to police within 24 hours","Wait until the renewal","Notify the DFO within 7 days",
 "Section 26 requires written notification of changed application information within 30 days.")
Q("A person who controlled a firearm learns that it was destroyed. Under section 120(11), when must the destruction be reported?",
 "No later than 24 hours after learning","No later than 7 days after learning","No later than 30 days after learning","At the next licence renewal",
 "Loss, theft and destruction all trigger reporting within 24 hours after the person becomes aware.")
Q("An owner is not carrying a firearm when an identified authorised official makes a general section 106 request to produce it for inspection. What period does that section allow?",
 "Within seven days","Immediately","Within 24 hours","Within 30 days",
 "Subject to section 107, section 106 requires production within seven days. The rule for a firearm being carried is different.")
Q("A licence holder's physical address changes. What period applies to the written notification under section 25?",
 "Within 30 days","Within 7 days","Within 90 days","At the next renewal",
 "Written notification must be given within 30 days of the change. It is not left until renewal.")
Q("A person knows where a firearm is that is not in anyone's lawful possession. How quickly does section 120(2) require them to tell police?",
 "Without delay","Within 24 hours","Within 7 days","Within 48 hours",
 "The statutory wording is without delay. The 24 hour rule is a separate duty for loss or theft of a firearm the person controlled.")
Q("A person's careless shooting smashes a neighbour's window. Which section 120(3) offence most directly fits the result?",
 "Negligent use causing damage","Discharge in a public place","Handling while intoxicated","Pointing a firearm at a person",
 "Section 120(3)(a) covers injury or property damage caused by negligent use of a firearm.")
Q("A person handles a firearm with reckless disregard for nearby people, although nobody is hurt. Which section 120(3) prohibition can apply without any injury?",
 "Handling likely to endanger others","Negligent use causing injury","Discharge without good reason","Failure to report a loss in 24 hours",
 "Section 120(3)(b) prohibits handling likely to endanger safety or property, or with reckless disregard for them.")
Q("A person leaves a loaded rifle propped against a car while children play nearby and does nothing to prevent danger. Which section 120(3) failure is described?",
 "Failing to take reasonable precautions","Negligent use causing an injury to others","Discharging it recklessly in public","Pointing it without good reason",
 "Section 120(3)(c) specifically covers failure to take reasonable precautions in a risky situation with a loaded firearm.")
Q("A person handles a firearm after drinking several beers. Which separate section 120 prohibition is most directly relevant?",
 "Handling it while under the influence","Negligent discharge of the firearm","Reckless endangerment of others","Supplying it to an unlicensed person",
 "Section 120(4) prohibits handling a firearm while under the influence of a substance with an intoxicating or narcotic effect.")
Q("An applicant deliberately hides a past conviction on the licence form. Which documentary offence is described?",
 "Giving false or misleading information","Altering a licence document by hand","Using another person's licence card","Making a false entry in a register",
 "Section 120(9)(f) prohibits giving knowingly false, incorrect or misleading information in an application.")
Q("A licence holder changes a printed detail on the card without the Registrar's permission. Which documentary offence is described?",
 "Unauthorised alteration of a document","Knowingly false application information","Failure to report a lost card","Allowing the card to wear out",
 "Unauthorised additions, alterations or erasures on competency or licensing documents are prohibited.")
Q("A buyer presents a relative's licence as though it lets the buyer acquire the firearm. Which documentary offence is described?",
 "Using someone else's document","Altering the relative's licence card","Making a false application","Failing to carry one's own licence",
 "Section 120(9)(e) prohibits using another person's document to procure possession.")
Q("A dealer invents a sale and writes it into the records the Act requires the dealer to keep. Which documentary offence is described?",
 "A false entry in a required register","Dealing in firearms without a licence","Altering a licence card by hand","Reporting a firearm sale late",
 "Section 120(9)(g) prohibits making a false entry in a register required under the Act.")
Q("As a joke, a person aims a realistic toy gun that looks like a real one at a friend. Which prohibition is directly relevant?",
 "Pointing it without good reason","No offence, since it is not real","Negligent discharge","Possessing an unlicensed firearm",
 "Section 120(6) includes objects likely to be taken for firearms. A joke does not by itself give good reason.")
Q("A person fires shots into the air at a street party in town. Which offence most directly applies?",
 "Discharge in public without good reason","Negligent use causing property damage","No offence, as nobody was hurt","Pointing a firearm without good reason",
 "Section 120(7) prohibits firing a firearm in a built-up area or public place without good reason.")
Q("An owner lets a visibly drunk friend hold a loaded pistol. Which prohibition concerns handing over control?",
 "Giving control to an impaired person","Negligent discharge of a firearm","Supplying a prohibited firearm to him","No offence if the owner stays nearby",
 "Section 120(5) prohibits giving control of a firearm to a person known, or who should reasonably be known, to be under the influence.")
Q("A seller hands a firearm to a buyer who has no lawful authority to possess it and no exception applies. Which offence is involved?",
 "Supplying someone not allowed to have it","Dealing in firearms without a permit","Failing to report the transfer in time","No offence, since the buyer paid for it",
 "Section 120(10)(a) prohibits giving possession to someone who is not allowed under the Act to possess the firearm.")
Q("A final section 102 declaration of unfitness is made. What happens from that date to the person's competency certificates, licences, permits and authorisations?",
 "They cease to be valid","They stay valid until expiry","They are suspended for 60 days","Only the main licence lapses",
 "Section 104(1) makes them cease to be valid from the date of the declaration or conviction.")
Q("A person has just become unfit under the Act and still holds the documents, firearms and ammunition covered by section 104. What immediate deadline applies?",
 "Hand them in to police within 24 hours","Hand them in within 7 days","Dispose of them within 60 days","Hand them in only after any appeal",
 "Section 104(2) requires surrender to the nearest police station within 24 hours.")
Q("A person declared unfit has filed an appeal and asks whether filing restores the right to possess firearms. What is the rule?",
 "Unfitness stays until the appeal ends","Filing restores the right to possess","Possession resumes after 30 days","One firearm may be kept in the meantime",
 "Unfitness remains in effect pending finalisation of the appeal under section 104(1)(b).")
Q("After handing in firearms following unfitness, the former owner wants to sell them. What restriction governs the next step?",
 "Through a dealer or as the Registrar says","A private sale to any licensed buyer","Giving them to a licensed family member","Leaving them with police indefinitely",
 "Section 104 requires disposal through a dealer or as the Registrar determines, within the applicable period. Surrender does not allow a private handover.")
Q("A person complying with section 104 hands over every firearm and all ammunition but keeps the paperwork the Act issued to them. What has been omitted?",
 "All competency and licensing documents","The firearm safe from the house","The keys to the empty firearm safe","Their identity document and passport",
 "The surrender duty includes all competency certificates, licences, permits and authorisations issued to the person under the Act.")
Q("A person declared unfit surrenders documents and ammunition but keeps an ordinary firearm because it played no part in the incident. What has been omitted?",
 "Every firearm they hold","Only firearms used in the incident","The holster for that firearm","Nothing, as only the incident firearm counts",
 "The duty covers all firearms in the person's possession, not only one involved in the event.")
Q("A person declared unfit surrenders every firearm and document but keeps some cartridges in a drawer. What has been omitted?",
 "All remaining ammunition","Only loaded magazines","The cleaning equipment","Only rounds above 200",
 "Section 104(2) also requires surrender of all ammunition in the person's possession.")
Q("A person asks where the section 104 surrender of documents, firearms and ammunition must be made. Which destination does the Act specify?",
 "The nearest police station","A licensed dealer","The Appeal Board","The Registrar's head office",
 "Section 104 specifies the nearest police station, not a dealer or another private recipient.")
Q("An identified authorised official stops a person carrying a firearm and asks for the document that authorises it. What duty applies under section 107?",
 "Produce it for inspection on request","Produce it within seven days","Refuse until a lawyer is present","Show an identity document only",
 "A person carrying a firearm must produce its licence, permit or authorisation for inspection when asked. The general seven-day period does not apply.")
Q("During a lawful section 107 check, the official asks the carrier who they are. Which duty addresses that request?",
 "Identify themselves forthwith","Identify themselves within seven days","Show only the firearm licence","Decline unless under arrest",
 "The person must identify themselves forthwith to the official's satisfaction.")
Q("The carrier has produced the licence, but the authorised official also wants to examine the pistol itself. What further section 107 duty applies?",
 "Hand over the firearm for inspection","Bring it to a station within seven days","Refuse, as the licence is enough","Let only a DFO inspect it",
 "The person must also produce the firearm for inspection when requested under section 107.")
Q("Someone claims section 107 inspection powers but has not shown who they are or any appointment certificate. Which safeguard does the Act require?",
 "The official must show their authority","The person must comply regardless","Only uniformed police may make it","A court warrant must be shown first",
 "The official exercising these powers must identify themselves and produce the appointment certificate or authorisation.")
Q("After considering a lawful appeal, the Appeal Board leaves the challenged ruling unchanged. Which statutory outcome is this?",
 "Confirming the decision","Varying it","Reversing it","Sending it back to the DFO",
 "Section 133 allows the Board to confirm a decision.")
Q("The Appeal Board modifies part of a challenged ruling while keeping the rest. Which statutory power describes this?",
 "Varying the decision","Confirming it","Reversing it in full","Setting it aside entirely",
 "Section 133 allows the Board to vary the decision, not only to accept or reject it as a whole.")
Q("The Appeal Board overturns the challenged refusal. Which statutory outcome is described?",
 "Reversing the decision","Confirming it","Varying part of it","Postponing it",
 "Reversing a decision is one of the powers listed in section 133.")
Q("An appeal notice arrives after the ordinary period, with a substantiated explanation why it could not be sent on time. What may the Board do under regulation 91?",
 "Condone the late submission on good cause","Reject it automatically for lateness","Accept it only if the Registrar agrees","Refer it to the High Court",
 "The Board may condone late submission on good cause. Lateness is not excused merely because the person wants to appeal.")
Q("A firearm is found on specified premises, and after reasonable efforts to link it to someone else, the State asks the court to infer who had it. Which provision is concerned?",
 "The section 117 presumption","The section 118 presumption","The section 119 presumption","Direct eyewitness evidence",
 "Section 117 provides evidential presumptions about possession. Its conditions matter, and contrary evidence raising reasonable doubt can defeat it.")
Q("An authorised possessor has not produced an allegedly missing firearm within seven days of a police request, and no contrary evidence raises reasonable doubt. Which evidential provision addresses this?",
 "Section 118, on failure to report","Section 117, on possession","Direct evidence from a witness","A conclusive rule that cannot be rebutted",
 "Section 118 provides the specified inference relating to loss, theft or destruction and failure to report. It is not irrebuttable.")
Q("A charge concerns failure to take reasonable steps to stop unlawful firearms being brought onto controlled premises. Which statutory presumption addresses that duty?",
 "Section 119","Section 117","Section 140","Section 120",
 "Section 119 addresses failure to take reasonable steps, subject to its evidential qualifications.")
Q("A witness personally saw the theft and describes what happened, instead of the court inferring it from non-production. What kind of proof is this?",
 "Direct first-hand evidence","A section 118 presumption","A section 117 presumption","Hearsay from the owner's family",
 "First-hand testimony describes the event directly. It is different from relying on a statutory presumption, though its reliability must still be assessed.")
Q("A person reports in person where an unlawfully held firearm is, under section 120(2). What must the police official hand over immediately?",
 "Written proof that the report was made","A case number for the report sent later","Nothing until an investigation opens","A copy of the officer's notebook",
 "For such a report, section 120(2)(b) requires written proof that the report was made. A reference number is used for telephonic or similar reports.")
Q("A person makes the section 120(2) report by telephone. What acknowledgement does the Act specify for that kind of report?",
 "An official reference number","A written receipt posted within 7 days","Nothing, as phone reports are informal","A court docket number",
 "For a telephonic or similar report, the official must provide the official reference number.")
Q("A firearm is stolen while its owner is visiting another town. Under section 120(11), which police station should receive the report?",
 "The one nearest to where it was stolen","The one nearest to the owner's home","The one that processed the licence","Any station within 30 days",
 "The statute specifies the police station nearest to the place where the loss, theft or destruction occurred.")
Q("A firearm theft happened overnight, but its lawful possessor discovers it the next morning. What event starts the section 120(11) reporting period?",
 "When the possessor became aware of it","The estimated time of the theft","When the insurer is told about it","Midnight on the day it happened",
 "The 24 hour period runs from becoming aware of the loss, theft or destruction, not from an unknown time when it happened.")
Q("An heir wants to keep an inherited ordinary firearm as a usable firearm of their own. Which section 148 route must be followed?",
 "Applying for the right licence or permit","Relying on the will as authority","Re-registering it in the estate's name","Continuing under the deceased's licence",
 "The heir must apply for the appropriate licence, permit or authorisation. Inheritance is not a licence to possess.")
Q("An heir does not want an inherited firearm to stay capable of firing and plans to keep it as an ornament. Which section 148 option fits?",
 "Lawful deactivation under the Act","Removing the firing pin themselves","Keeping it under the deceased's licence","Storing it unloaded indefinitely",
 "Section 148 allows deactivation in accordance with the Act. This is a regulated process, not permission for improvised alteration.")
Q("An heir does not wish to keep an inherited firearm and prefers to pass it on rather than have it deactivated. Which statutory option applies?",
 "Dispose of it in terms of the Act","Give it to anyone who wants it","Hand it to a relative to keep","Sell it privately with a receipt",
 "The firearm must be disposed of in terms of the Act, so both the recipient and the transfer route must be lawful.")
Q("An heir needs a reasonable time to arrange lawful disposal and seeks a time-limited permission under section 148(2). Which route is relevant?",
 "A section 21 temporary authorisation","A section 13 licence","A dealer's licence","A regulation 86(4) storage authorisation",
 "The Registrar may issue a section 21 temporary authorisation to give an heir reasonable time to dispose of the firearm.")
Q("A person with no applicable authorisation walks into a stadium declared a firearm-free zone with a holstered pistol. What is breached?",
 "The ban on bringing firearms into the zone","Nothing, as a licence overrides the zone notice","The ban on storing ammunition there","Only a rule against discharging it",
 "A normal licence does not override the prohibition on carrying firearms or ammunition into a declared zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("A person with no applicable authorisation keeps cartridges in a locker inside a declared firearm-free zone. Which section 140 restriction applies?",
 "The ban on storing ammunition there","None, since it is only ammunition","The ban on carrying a firearm in","Only the venue's own house rules",
 "Section 140 prohibits storing firearms or ammunition in the zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("A venue manager knowingly lets guests bring firearms into a declared firearm-free zone, without any authority to do so. Which section 140 restriction is directly relevant?",
 "The ban on allowing firearms to be present","None, as the manager carries nothing","Only the rule on discharging firearms","The rule on reporting any firearms found",
 "Section 140 separately prohibits allowing firearms or ammunition to be in the zone without applicable authorisation.")
Q("A police official lawfully finds a firearm in a declared firearm-free zone in breach of the notice. Which section 140 power concerns taking it into custody?",
 "Seizure of the firearm","A seven-day production notice","Cancelling the licence on the spot","Declaring the person unfit",
 "Section 140 permits seizure of firearms or ammunition present in breach of the zone notice, subject to the statutory conditions.")
assert len(ITEMS)==52
